  "slug": "report-technical-4b00",
  "summary": "Headline accuracy is giving way to scrutiny of confounds, held-out splits and measured targets in protein and peptide predictors.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is whether predictive scores for biological sequences track the property of interest once confounds are controlled. The antimicrobial peptide protocol enforces homology separation and length matching, and finds that length alone separates classes. ProtCOVER applies family-level conformal calibration to variant effect predictors on unseen protein families, with density-ratio reweighting under descriptor shift. The ESMC interpretability benchmark tests protein language model internals against residue-level curated annotations, and its conclusions on depth, probes, neurons and SAEs depend on that validation setup. Match and Correct probes OpenFold's Evoformer layers for distance decodability and triangle-inequality violations. MADM and PhageLysClass extend architecture scope through PLM embeddings for developability and phage lytic protein classification. What is new is explicit scrutiny of what these models exploit and what they are validated against, rather than headline accuracy alone.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-23"
}
